WEBVTT

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:04.800
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> The perception that being happy is all about thinking positively

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.380
creates a burden of expectation on people.

00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:12.475
It can mean that negative emotions such as anger, sadness, envy,

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:16.616
and guilt are seen as not just undesirable, but unhealthy.

00:00:17.176 --> 00:00:20.328
In my book The Positive Power of Negative Emotions,

00:00:20.714 --> 00:00:23.295
I argue that these emotions are not only normal,

00:00:23.629 --> 00:00:28.290
but they may also be pathways to deeper and richer forms of wellbeing,

00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:30.211
if we can only harness them.

00:00:30.778 --> 00:00:33.518
Firstly, anger can be a force for good.

00:00:34.043 --> 00:00:39.020
Much of what we label ‘anger’ is actually frustration that life isn’t going our way –

00:00:39.621 --> 00:00:44.023
our irritation at a traffic jam or an uncooperative laptop.

00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:45.718
We can learn from it:

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.171
our annoyance at the traffic may lead to the realization

00:00:49.305 --> 00:00:52.613
that we are over-stressed and prompt us to make changes.

00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:56.641
When anger is truly about a sense of moral injustice,

00:00:56.781 --> 00:01:01.659
it can be harnessed for good, as the history of civil rights and feminism shows.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:06.310
There’s a distinction between feeling angry and being angry,

00:01:06.809 --> 00:01:09.231
and this is important to teach our children.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.325
If you feel angry, step back and examine why,

00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:15.942
then act to solve the problem.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:21.405
But being angry is almost always unhelpful and destructive.

00:01:22.064 --> 00:01:24.890
It’s very important to tease those two apart.

00:01:25.703 --> 00:01:30.252
Next, it’s important to realize that boredom unlocks creativity.

00:01:30.812 --> 00:01:33.659
Being bored clears space for reflection.

00:01:34.204 --> 00:01:37.393
Sitting on a train staring out of the window lets you reflect:

00:01:37.606 --> 00:01:40.455
How am I? Am I living the life I wanted?

00:01:41.182 --> 00:01:45.272
The theory of relativity came to Einstein while he was daydreaming.

00:01:46.093 --> 00:01:51.294
Until recently it was thought that the brain winds down when not engaged in a task,

00:01:51.594 --> 00:01:56.946
but neuroscientists now believe that there is a type of interconnected brain activity

00:01:57.239 --> 00:02:00.307
that clicks into gear when we are not focused,

00:02:00.613 --> 00:02:03.755
and which is important for creativity and innovation.

00:02:04.954 --> 00:02:08.935
The third thing to remember is that guilt motivates us.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.432
Guilt can serve as a moral emotion,

00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:16.516
a motivating force that helps us to become better people.

00:02:17.016 --> 00:02:23.616
We can turn it into a learning experience if we pause to unpick exactly why we are feeling guilty:

00:02:24.135 --> 00:02:25.407
Where did we go wrong?

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:27.669
Why did we behave the way we did?

00:02:28.289 --> 00:02:33.041
It’s only when we reflect on our errors that we develop and grow as people.

00:02:33.507 --> 00:02:36.445
Don’t beat yourself up about a past action,

00:02:36.812 --> 00:02:39.924
but take future opportunities to do better.

00:02:40.677 --> 00:02:43.938
Fourthly, envy helps us achieve our goals.

00:02:44.477 --> 00:02:50.616
There are two types of envy: the hostile and corrosive kind that social media often induces,

00:02:51.162 --> 00:02:57.330
and the envy which is similar to admiration and inspires us to lift ourselves up to that level.

00:02:58.063 --> 00:03:03.835
Sometimes, it’s only by envying someone else that you clarify your own values and goals.

00:03:04.657 --> 00:03:08.779
There are two ways to make our envy a useful motivational force.

00:03:09.205 --> 00:03:13.016
The first is to focus on ourselves rather than the target:

00:03:13.389 --> 00:03:16.001
How can we attain what they have?

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:19.854
The second is to realize when the goal is unattainable

00:03:20.068 --> 00:03:22.936
and turn the envy towards an achievable outcome.

00:03:23.563 --> 00:03:27.030
Envying someone’s material possessions is understandable,

00:03:27.642 --> 00:03:32.579
but studies show that greater wealth does not generally lead to happiness.

00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:36.980
It is better to envy those with greater ‘social capital’ –

00:03:37.246 --> 00:03:40.499
large friendship networks and a sense of community.

00:03:41.399 --> 00:03:44.484
Next, loneliness needn’t be painful.

00:03:44.944 --> 00:03:50.421
Sometimes, loneliness acts as a prompt to reach out and connect with more people.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.266
At other times, it’s a matter of reframing loneliness as solitude,

00:03:55.646 --> 00:03:57.134
which is something quite different.

00:03:57.594 --> 00:04:02.060
This involves learning to appreciate the value and beauty of aloneness,

00:04:02.303 --> 00:04:06.078
rather than trying to ‘solve’ it through a continual search for company.

00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:09.670
Finally, sadness gives us space.

00:04:10.337 --> 00:04:18.759
There’s an increasing tendency among doctors to view sadness as a disorder and medicalize it as mild depression.

00:04:19.319 --> 00:04:26.666
However, the ‘regular’ levels of sadness most of us feel at some point can serve valuable functions.

00:04:27.338 --> 00:04:30.231
The pain of sadness has a protective role.

00:04:30.557 --> 00:04:36.014
We withdraw; we signal our sadness to loved ones so they care for us.

00:04:36.613 --> 00:04:42.980
Then, we begin to recuperate by re-evaluating the circumstances that brought us to this low ebb.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.587
Sadness can create the time and space for this essential process of questioning,

00:04:49.007 --> 00:04:52.627
which is why we tend to experience epiphanies at low points.